  {
    "episode_id": 91897,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91897.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "well i mean look when it comes to florida i've always said we have the best election laws in the country we learned our lessons twenty twenty two years ago now and every year we've gotten better and it's real simple first we've had a very robust mail-in balloting our process for a long time That's been refined. Voters have to request their absentee ballot. We don't do mass mailouts. Voters can only get one. They have to sign the envelope. If you get an absentee ballot but you decide to vote at the polls, you have to bring the ballot to the polls and turn it over to the poll watcher. So we believe in ballot security and ballot integrity. Number two, we've had, obviously, name ID, a voter ID in our state for a very long time. Ever since I went to college in Florida, that's 25 years ago, we've had voter ID. Three, we have a process where when it comes to counting ballots, our supervisors are allowed to actually tabulate mail-in ballots ahead of time. So that way, when they're waiting for election day ballots to come in, they do the final tabulations. and everybody knows who won elections in florida by basically ten o'clock at night it makes sense every state should follow it i wish they would but you know the other forty nine they gotta make their own decisions",
